meta {
  name: 9c2. Verify X-Userinfo spoofing is rejected (forged identity → 401, not 403)
  type: http
  seq: 25
  tags: [
    requires-nifi
  ]
}

get {
  url: {{apisixApiUrl}}/v1/datasets/{{sagaDataSetId}}/{{sagaSlug}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Userinfo: eyJzdWIiOiJmb3JnZWQtYWRtaW4tMTIzNDUifQ
}

tests {
  test("A forged X-Userinfo cannot impersonate a user — gateway strips it before OPA (401, not 403)", function() {
    // Since unauth_action=pass lets anonymous requests reach OPA, and OPA's trust model keys identity
    // off X-Userinfo, the gateway's serverless-pre-function MUST clear any client-supplied X-Userinfo
    // before openid-connect/OPA. The forged header carries sub="forged-admin-12345".
    //
    // The status code is the discriminator: when the strip works, OPA sees NO userinfo → anonymous →
    // 401. If the strip were dropped/broken, the forged header would reach OPA → it would be treated
    // as an (authenticated-but-unknown) caller → 403. Asserting exactly 401 turns a future regression
    // (plugin removed, Lua typo, phase reorder) into a red test instead of a silent identity bypass.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(401);
  });
}

docs {
  **Saga step 9c2** — Security regression guard for X-Userinfo spoofing.

  With `unauth_action: "pass"`, anonymous requests now reach OPA, and OPA decides authorization from the
  `X-Userinfo` header (the decoded `sub`). A `serverless-pre-function` (rewrite phase, priority 10000,
  before openid-connect at 2599) clears any client-supplied `X-Userinfo`/`X-Access-Token`/`X-Id-Token`,
  so a client cannot forge an identity. This test sends an unauthenticated request with a **forged**
  `X-Userinfo` to the protected dataset (openDataAccess=false at this step) and asserts the gateway
  denies with **401** — proving the forged header was stripped and never reached OPA (a non-stripped
  forged header would yield 403).

  **Precondition:** runs after 9c while the dataset is still protected (before 9f toggles open).
}
